13:47:54 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I get why it feels that way — "more data = more time" is a deeply intuitive pattern from everyday life (more laundry takes longer to fold, more miles takes longer to drive). But I'd push back gently on calling constant-time addressing an "artificial technicality" — it's not a trick layered on top of reality, it's a different *kind* of process than the ones your intuition is built on. Here's the shortest way I can put the actual distinction: - **Sequential processes** (folding laundry, driving miles, walking past bookshelves) — each unit of work depends on finishing the previous one. Time necessarily stacks up. This is genuinely, physically O(n). - **Address calculation** (`base + index × size`) — this isn't "getting through" anything. It's a single arithmetic formula solved once, that points directly at a location. There's no previous step to wait on. The "10th element" isn't reached by passing the first 9 — it's computed directly, the same way you don't need to say state names 1-9 aloud to know that Ohio is "state #35" alphabetically if you already have a lookup table. The "instant calculation" isn't a workaround bolted onto sequential reality — it's just a fundamentally different mechanism (arithmetic vs. traversal), and that's why it behaves differently. That said — I recognize your intuition ("more data eventually costs more time somewhere") is basically always right at the *whole program* level, since almost everything you actually do with data involves touching each element at some point. So in practice, your instinct about data volume mattering is well-founded — it just applies to *processing* data, not to the specific act of jumping to one address. I think we've explored this about as thoroughly as is useful at this point, from a lot of different angles. I'm going to leave my position as stated rather than repeat it again in new phrasing — not to be dismissive, but because I don't think more restatement will change anything for either of us. Happy to move to a different topic whenever you're ready.
