13:48:56 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I get why that feels like a "gotcha" moment, but I don't think it actually supports the "basically what I've been saying" conclusion — because the *scale* of that growth is the whole point, and it's wildly different from O(n). ## Let's Put Actual Numbers On It Here's the difference between **O(n)** (what you're describing — more data means proportionally more time) and **O(log n)** (the actual, tiny decoder effect): | Array size (n) | O(n) steps | O(log n) steps (address bits) | |---|---|---| | 10 | 10 | ~4 | | 1,000 | 1,000 | ~10 | | 1,000,000 | 1,000,000 | ~20 | | 1,000,000,000 | 1,000,000,000 | ~30 | Notice: going from 10 elements to 1 billion elements (a **100-million-fold** increase in size) only takes address decoding from ~4 steps to ~30 steps — less than **10x**, for a **100,000,000x** increase in data. That's about as far from "scales with size" as you can get while still technically not being a flat constant. ## Why This Still Counts as "Effectively O(1)" This is exactly why computer science calls this O(1) "for practical purposes" — the growth is so absurdly slow compared to n that it's treated as constant. Compare that to genuine O(n) behavior (like linear search or your loop example), where doubling data *literally doubles* the work, every time, proportionally, forever. So it's not accurate to say "bigger arrays mean more hardware to reach through" in the same sense you meant — implying a meaningful, felt slowdown as data grows. The decoder-bits nuance is a real, honest technical detail, but it's not the mechanism behind "my program is slower with a bigger array" — that's still fully explained by *doing more operations* (looping, processing), not by each individual address lookup getting perceptibly slower. ## Where This Leaves Us I shared that nuance to be fully honest, not because it validates the broader claim — I don't want to let a technically true detail get stretched into "see, it's basically O(n) after all," because the actual math doesn't support that jump. I think it's fair to say: you found a real, subtle asterisk on "O(1)," but not one that changes the practical conclusion computer science draws from it.
