13:50:07 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a really important question, and the good news is: no, this doesn't open some slippery slope. Your general instinct — "bigger data usually costs more somewhere" — is actually still correct almost all the time. Let me draw a clear boundary so this doesn't spiral into doubting everything. ## The General Rule Still Holds **Default assumption: bigger data = more cost.** This is true for: - Loops (O(n)) - Nested loops (O(n²)) - Sorting (O(n log n)) - Searching by value (O(n)) - Copying, filtering, summing, printing — basically anything that *touches* each element This is like 95% of what you'll actually write and reason about. Your instinct here is good and should stay your default. ## The Exception Is Narrow and Specific Array index access (`arr[i]`) is a **specific, named exception** — not a gateway to doubting everything. It's an exception precisely *because* it doesn't touch other elements at all; it computes a location directly. That's a special property of that one operation, not a general principle that other things might secretly share. A short list of the *rare* things that share this property: - Array index access — O(1) - Hash table lookup — O(1) average - That's... basically the core list for typical coding Everything else you'd intuitively expect to scale with size (loops, searches, sorts, most data structure operations like linked list traversal, tree operations, etc.) **does** scale with size, exactly as your instincts say. ## A Simple Rule to Keep Your Instincts Intact If an operation needs to **look at or pass through other elements** to do its job → assume it scales with size. If an operation goes **directly** to one spot via a formula (index math, hash math) → it's a candidate for O(1). This isn't a reason to distrust your broader instincts at all — if anything, treat array/hash access as **the exception you now know about**, not a crack that undermines the rule. Your instinct "more data, more cost" remains the correct default assumption for basically everything else you'll encounter.
